 ## Overview
-Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF is a method for learning neural volumetric videos of dynamic humans from sparse view videos in minutes with competitive visual quality [S1]. It addresses the slow convergence of per-scene optimization methods by decomposing the canonical human body into multiple anatomical parts, assigning each part its own Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)-augmented NeRF network with varying resolutions to efficiently distribute representational power [S1]. Unlike single-resolution voxelized representations or continuous implicit functions that treat the entire body uniformly, this method leverages a 2D motion parameterization scheme on a parametric mesh (SMPL) to accelerate deformation field learning [S1].
+Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF is a method for learning neural volumetric videos of dynamic humans from sparse view videos in minutes with competitive visual quality [S1]. It addresses the challenge of quickly reconstructing free-viewpoint videos of dynamic humans by decomposing the canonical human body into multiple anatomical parts, assigning each part its own Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)-augmented NeRF network [S1]. This approach efficiently distributes representational power to handle varying shape and texture complexity across different body parts (e.g., face vs. torso) and accelerates training convergence compared to a single unified NeRF [S1]. It builds on [Voxel-based Representation](10852-voxel-based-representation.md) by using a structured set of voxelized NeRF networks rather than a single global field.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- A sequence of sparse multi-view videos of a dynamic human.
-- SMPL parameters (pose $\rho$, shape $\beta$) for each frame, or a pre-estimated SMPL mesh sequence [S1].
-- Camera intrinsics and extrinsics for the input views [S1].
+*   Sparse multi-view video frames of a dynamic human.
+*   SMPL parameters for each frame: pose $\rho$, shape $\beta$, and camera extrinsics/intrinsics.
+*   For each query point $\mathbf{x}$ at frame $t$, the method requires finding the nearest surface point on the posed SMPL mesh.
 
 **Outputs:**
-- A set of $K$ MHE-augmented NeRF networks, one for each human part $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$, representing the canonical space [S1].
-- A latent embedding $l_t$ for each frame $t$ [S1].
-- Rendered novel view images for any camera pose and time $t$ [S1].
+*   Density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$ for any query point $\mathbf{x}$ in space and time $t$.
+*   Rendered novel view images via volume rendering.
 
